# Canonical Doc source host: independent first review

Base 0d80fe6289346a1b8481b03b149d5b35af90d68d plus the exact twelve pending files in overlay-manifest.json. Source copies are in source/. Dependencies were extracted from that Git base into workspace/ and the overlay applied; the obsolete migration/authored/13-reproducibility.nepld was removed in the isolated copy. Only an independent probe binary source was added to the isolated tools/src/bin directory. No live production, manuscript or Git changes by this reviewer. Later root policy/manuscript additions are not silently part of this frozen code snapshot.

Two concrete path issues require correction before final approval of the public source-selection adapter:

1. On Windows, canonical::check accepts registry.source=doc/real./input.nepld when the only actual directory is doc/real. It resolves the same physical file as doc/real/input.nepld but receives a distinct lowercased registry key and metadata path. The metadata/content exact check passes because the projection is generated with that spelling. This is a portable-path/identity ambiguity, not an observed escape outside the supplied root. Results preserve both spellings. The intermediate trailing-space spelling failed on this platform; that failure is not generalized to every Windows namespace API.
2. An actual doc/hash#name.nepld file and its correctly generated projection pass canonical::check, while canonical::html with the same registry fails InvalidInputPath. The canonical path predicate permits #/% while the reused pages::input_path explicitly excludes them. The selection registry should use a path domain compatible with both promised operations. The current real chapter13 paths avoid these cases, but the new public adapter accepts them.

The findings were sent to root with original inputs and actual public API results. Internal Windows directory junctions were created independently and correctly rejected by symlink_metadata.is_symlink in this Rust implementation; do not label them an unverified escape. The Unix-only symlink test was not executed on this Windows host. Repository containment is checked by the existing canonicalized local_path helper, and the provided root remains the caller's explicit authority. TOCTOU/adversarial concurrent filesystem replacement was not simulated.

Positive/negative API evidence: exact generated text, stale Markdown, source-only edit, alias-byte whitespace edit, missing alias section, malformed Doc source, duplicate JSON and unknown registry field, checked HTML, and existing-output preservation all behaved as expected. The independent tiny original uses Ruby and Anno with an explicit visible-text expectation. Each mutation verifies that check does not adopt or rewrite the input. The four existing canonical native tests also passed in the fixed snapshot. Original and expanded probe source, commands, logs and the latest executable hash are retained. The expanded probe uses a new fixture without the original junction; its missing-linked-path result is not substituted for the earlier actual-junction rejection.

Real chapter13 CLI checks were independently executed against the same isolated source and unique target: doc-canonical --check and doc-canonical html both exited 0. The migrated .nepld is byte-identical to the prior reviewed candidate (SHA31486ad75e0776d55cc12b2a9b5af2840780ad7812456d9c222a90fd8ab495a8). Generated Markdown metadata binds the actual source and six-alias input bytes. The HTML/CSS output file hashes and physical/logical origins are checked against its emitted manifest. These results establish generation/metadata consistency, not new semantic/human approval or GitHub's automatic-anchor behavior. Existing chapter13 content/anchor reviews remain separate; this review does not independently recertify all old headings or authorize canonical cutover.

Other implementation observations: generate() extracts the existing annotated write pipeline without replacing parsing or adopting golden output. Check regenerates and compares exact bytes, including metadata and line endings. HTML reads canonical Doc while retaining the old Markdown path as the logical link namespace; its physical input remains the Doc path. It reuses the existing page-set generator and crate-private write_generated only on that generator's successful output. create_new/create_dir prevents overwriting an existing output; partial filesystem artifacts may remain after a later I/O failure, but no successful return or completion manifest is fabricated. HTML intentionally does not require the generated Markdown file to exist; it is not an implicit --check call.

The CLI uses its compiled repository root, and native CI explicitly runs both regeneration check and HTML export before tracked cleanliness. The source-selection registry has strict typed serde boundaries, duplicate-JSON validation, version/count limits and finite reads. It does not authenticate human review, legacy anchor completeness or arbitrary raw alias semantics merely by being present. No new core/runtime schema/ABI dependency was introduced. Final approval remains pending the two reported path corrections and any later integration delta.
